WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.180
The first trailer for director Todd Phillips'
Joker has arrived, and the Joaquin Phoenix-led

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:08.260
origin story looks, in a word, terrifying.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:12.280
Here are some of the small details you may
have missed in one of the most interesting

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:13.720
trailers of the year so far.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:18.280
Struggling comedian Arthur Fleck is first
seen sitting in the office of his therapist,

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.680
who asks him if it helps to have someone to
talk to.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.150
He doesn't answer.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:27.320
His first spoken lines in the trailer, heard
in voiceover as he makes his lonely way up

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.740
a set of stone steps, makes reference to his
mother.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.140
"She told me I had a purpose.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.360
To bring laughter and joy to the world."

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:42.360
A part-time clown and struggling comedian,
Fleck appears to have taken her advice to

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:46.660
heart, but there are clues that their relationship
isn't necessarily the healthiest.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:51.420
Fleck is seen bathing his mother as if she
were a child and dancing with her as if she

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:52.000
were his wife.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.820
These are the only times she appears in the
spot and the only scenes in which Fleck looks

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:57.940
genuinely happy.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:02.880
It can be implied from the therapist's question
that Fleck would never share his inner darkness

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:03.400
with his mother.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.479
There are also hints that his mother might
actually be the reason for Fleck's troubles.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:10.780
He appears to be filling a caretaker role
here.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.460
The specter of mental illness looms large
over the trailer.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:17.200
Early on, Fleck is seen scribbling in a notebook.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.580
The page is labeled "Jokes," but the sentiment
scrawled out in increasingly large letters

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:24.440
at its bottom isn't particularly funny.

00:01:24.869 --> 00:01:29.360
It may be that Fleck's mother's own struggles
with mental illness will have a part to play

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.900
here, further suggested by the fact that he
is seen visiting Arkham Hospital, where he

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:37.800
is visibly disturbed as a patient sharing
his elevator thrashes about on a stretcher.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.880
Who is he visiting there, if not dear old
Mom?

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:45.959
The very next shot sees him in the audience
at a comedy club called Pogo's, incidentally,

00:01:45.959 --> 00:01:51.099
the stage name of John Wayne Gacy, where we
hear the Joker's singularly unhinged cackle

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:53.400
coming out of Fleck's mouth for the first
time.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:57.140
Could his mother's decline as a result of
mental illness be what drives him over the

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:57.660
edge?

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:01.960
One very brief shot in the middle of the trailer
might grab your attention if you're a classic

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:02.820
film buff.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:07.700
An audience is seen gathering outside a concert
hall called Wayne Hall to catch a screening

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:09.740
of the Charlie Chaplin film Modern Times.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:14.760
The more familiar one is with Chaplin's life
and work, the more significant the shot feels.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:20.040
Though he was a pioneer of cinema comedy,
there was an underlying melancholy to Chaplin,

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.580
informed by his difficult upbringing.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:27.420
He had an absentee father and a mother who
was eventually committed to an asylum.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:30.220
Chaplin would eventually rise to fame behind
a pale-faced, clownish persona.

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:34.890
Even the titles and production histories of
some of his works are thematically tied to

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:35.880
the content of this film.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:40.260
There's The Circus, about a clown who can
only make people laugh unintentionally.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.360
During its filming, Chaplin's mother passed
and he went through a divorce.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.860
Then there's Limelight, a later work featuring
Chaplin as a washed-up comedian who literally

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:51.280
performs up until the moment of his death.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:53.120
And there's Laughing Gas.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.400
You can probably put that one together.

00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:00.080
And there's Modern Times itself, in which
Chaplin's beleaguered assembly line worker

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:03.120
is hospitalized after suffering a nervous
breakdown.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:07.980
Joker may be the first film to focus squarely
on the villain rather than Batman, but the

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:11.640
trailer goes out of its way to let us know
that Fleck's history with the Wayne family

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:13.220
will be long and tangled.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.900
While Phillips has insisted that his flick
will be a true standalone film with no sequels,

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.840
that doesn't mean he won't in some way explore
Fleck and Bruce Wayne's relationship to each

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:23.380
other.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:27.680
The trailer hints at the fact that Fleck may
set young Master Wayne on a dark path early

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:28.340
in life.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.230
During one sequence in which Fleck is shown
lovingly handling a gun, there's a talking

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:34.220
head on his television.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:38.300
"What kind of coward would do something that
cold blooded?

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:40.100
Someone who hides behind a mask."

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:44.620
This man happens to be none other than Thomas
Wayne, played by Brett Cullen.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:49.900
A later sequence finds Fleck outside of Wayne
Manor's gates, playfully engaging with young

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.280
Bruce, played by Dante Pereira-Olson.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:57.300
He then reaches through the gate's bars, pushing
the boy's lips up into a twisted smile.

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:00.740
Thomas Wayne will apparently serve as an antagonist
to Fleck.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:04.800
Will the comedian follow in the footsteps
of another cinematic Joker, becoming the one

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:08.240
to commit the crimes which result in the creation
of his nemesis?

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:13.740
There are few actors of greater stature than
Robert De Niro, and his casting in Joker can

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:16.400
certainly be seen as something of a coup for
the production.

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:18.740
It can also be seen as something more.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:23.400
Phillips has made no secret of the great Martin
Scorsese's influence on his film, and De Niro

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:27.920
has appeared in some of the director's greatest
works, including Taxi Driver, which features

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:32.040
iconic shots of De Niro's Travis Bickle striking
shirtless poses very similar to those struck

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:37.520
by Fleck in the trailer, and The King of Comedy,
which may be somewhat of a blueprint for Joker.

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:43.560
In Scorcese's film, De Niro portrays Rupert
Pupkin, an aspiring comedian with severe mental

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:44.620
health issues of his own.

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:49.740
A chance meeting with talk show host Jerry
Langford, played by Jerry Lewis, convinces

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:51.480
him that he's finally gotten his big break.

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:56.680
But when Langford shoots down his request
to appear on his show, Pupkin resorts to drastic

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:57.220
measures.

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:02.220
He kidnaps Langford with the help of an accomplice,
and for his ransom, convinces the authorities

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:06.120
to proceed with the taping of his show with
Pupkin as the opening performer.

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:11.480
He proceeds to deliver an absolutely killer
set, parlaying the appearance into nationwide

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:12.000
fame.

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:17.460
A result which, in true Scorsese fashion,
may very well have been all in his troubled

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:17.980
mind.

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:22.800
Considering that De Niro portrays a talk show
host in Joker, and that Fleck is seen waiting

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:27.520
in the wings of his show, it's safe to say
that a similar dynamic may come into play.

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:31.440
The trailer's closing shots feature Fleck
in full Joker mode.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.880
His hair is dyed a bright green, with makeup
in full effect.

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:38.900
There's a bouquet of fake flowers in hand
while he strides purposefully down what appears

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:43.440
to be one of Arkham Hospital's halls wearing
a less-than-mirthful expression.

00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:48.120
He's also seen in the same garb performing
a triumphant dance on the very stone steps

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:53.069
he's shown traversing at the beginning, and
his look is more "classic Joker" than at any

00:05:53.069 --> 00:05:53.980
point in the trailer.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:57.940
Hardcore DC Comics fans will have a special
appreciation for the look.

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:03.800
It's very similar to the Joker's design on
the cover of 1991's Robin II issue #1.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:08.840
The makeup is different, but it's obvious
that Joker's costume designers did their homework,

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:13.400
crafting a look more visually evocative of
the comics' Clown Prince of Crime than perhaps

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:15.180
any we've seen on film thus far.

